of ''wapper'', freq. of ''wap''<sup>1</sup>: see ''wap''<sup>1</sup>.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To incline to the one side and to the other alternately, as a wheel, top, spindle, or other rotating body when not properly balanced; move in the manner of a rotating disk when its plane vibrates from side to side; rock; vacillate.
{{smaller block|To ''wabble'' . . . [a low barbarous word]. ''Johnson'', Dict.

When . . . the top falls on to the table, . . . it falls into a certain oscillation, described by the expressive though inelegant word — ''wabbling.''
{{right|''H. Spencer'', First Principles, § 170.}}

It [a pendulum] should be symmetrical on each side of the middle plane of its vibration, or it will ''wobble.''
{{right|''Sir E. Beckett'', Clocks and Watches, p. 42.}}}}
Hence—'''2.''' To vacillate, vibrate, tremble, or exhibit unevenness, in senses other than mechanical. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|Ferri . . . made use of the tremolo upon every note, to such an extent that his whole singing was a bad ''wobbling'' trill. {{right|''Grove'', Dict. Music, III. 509.}}}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' To cause to wabble: as, to ''wabble''
